lked - used to produce shared data programs

syntax:

lked id file1 [args] [files] [libraries]

or

lked

description:

	The shared data region is the text of 'file1'. 'Id' is stored in
the a.out header and when a program with the shared data magic number (412)
is executed then the system looks for existing running programs
with this 'id' and links to the existing shared region, otherwise a new
one is created from the text area of 'file1'.
	Data and bss sections of 'file1' are not shared and neither is
any part of subsequent files, thus 'file1' should normally be first produced
by using ld -r. 'Args' are optional ld arguments as are 'libraries',
with the normal ld syntax.
	Executable output is always left in 'a.out'.

	Two system calls, pp and vv with syntax

	pp(&lock)
	vv(&lock)
	int lock;

	handle resource allocation for shared data programs.
	these correspond to the standard P & V synchronization primitives.
	lock initially contains a count of the maximum number
	of processes that can use the resource controlled by lock,
	at any one time. It is usually 1, thus programs requiring
	a resource should call pp first to ensure that they have
	private access to the resource, and afterwards call vv
	to release it for other processes.

bugs:
	There is no protection of shared data id's, so usually
	only the super user should be allowed to use them.
	i.e. a little care is required - but this
	generally is not a problem as we have only used
	shared data for system programs - batch subsystem
	- data base - etc

	Entry is a little tricky as the system will start 
executing the program at 0 of the shared data that is already loaded.
Therefore the first part of the shared data file should contain code
like
	.text
	jmp	start
	.data
start:	jsr	pc,_main
	.text
	etc....
	to ensure that all programs using the shared area are
	entered correctly.

	separate I/D space is NOT supported concurrently with shared data
